---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import StreamingMarkdownDemo from "../../components/demos/StreamingMarkdownDemo";
import StreamingMarkdownPlayground from "../../components/demos/StreamingMarkdownPlayground";
import ApiTable from "../../components/ApiTable.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Streaming Markdown" withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Streaming Markdown</h1>
    <p>
      Markdown from a model, rendered as real elements — <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>,
      <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>,
      <code>&lt;blockquote&gt;</code>, and Kernel's
      <a href="/components/code-block/">Code Block</a> for fences — while it is
      still arriving. Finished blocks never re-render, and half-written syntax
      never flashes on screen as raw asterisks and pipes.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-example">
      <StreamingMarkdownDemo client:visible />
    </div>

    <h2>Playground</h2>
    <p>Type Markdown into <code>content</code>, and toggle <code>streaming</code> to see how unfinished syntax is read.</p>
    <StreamingMarkdownPlayground client:visible />

    <h2>Props</h2>
    <ApiTable slug="streaming-markdown" />

    <h2>Stable streaming</h2>
    <p>
      The top level is split into blocks, and each block remembers the exact
      source slice it came from. Blocks are keyed by index and memoised on that
      slice, so a new chunk re-renders only the last block: earlier paragraphs,
      tables and code blocks keep their DOM nodes, their layout and the
      reader's text selection. Inside the live block, each word is its own
      span, also keyed by index, so words already on screen stay put and only
      the new ones mount — which is what the optional fade-in
      (<code>animate</code>) keys off. The fade is a short opacity and blur
      settle on the motion tokens, and it is off under reduced motion.
    </p>
    <p>
      While <code>streaming</code> is true, unfinished constructs render as
      their finished form would:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>an unterminated fence is an open code block, following its last line;</li>
      <li><code>**bold</code> with no closer yet is bold so far, and a marker with nothing after it yet is hidden;</li>
      <li><code>[text](https://exa</code> shows <em>text</em> and hides the half-written destination;</li>
      <li>a table renders as soon as its header row arrives, and a half row is a row;</li>
      <li>a last line that is only the start of a marker (<code>-</code>, <code>#</code>, <code>&gt;</code>) is held back until it means something.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In the elements package, assign <code>el.content</code> as chunks arrive.
      Unchanged blocks are left alone and the last one is patched in place,
      element by element and text node by text node — React's index-keyed
      reconciliation in miniature.
    </p>

    <h2>What it parses</h2>
    <p>
      A compact, dependency-free subset of CommonMark plus GitHub's tables:
      paragraphs, ATX headings, emphasis, strong, strikethrough, inline code,
      fenced code, nested ordered and unordered lists, blockquotes, links,
      autolinks (<code>&lt;https://…&gt;</code> and bare URLs) and horizontal
      rules. Deliberately left out: raw HTML (always text), indented code
      blocks, setext headings, reference-style links, footnotes and task-list
      checkboxes. <code>parseMarkdown</code> is exported from both packages if
      you want the tree itself.
    </p>

    <h2>Safety</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The parser produces data, never an HTML string, and the renderer builds
        elements from it — so model text can only ever become text. A
        <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;img onerror&gt;</code> in the
        content renders as those characters.
      </li>
      <li>
        Links keep only <code>http:</code>, <code>https:</code>,
        <code>mailto:</code> and relative destinations, with control characters
        stripped before the scheme is read. An unsafe link renders as its text.
        External links open in a new tab with
        <code>rel="noopener noreferrer"</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Images render as a link to the image and are never loaded. An image URL
        written by a model is a known way to leak a conversation to a
        third-party server the moment it renders.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Overriding elements</h2>
    <p>
      <code>components</code> replaces any element by tag — each override gets
      the props the default would have had, so spread them to keep Kernel's
      styling. <code>codeBlock</code> replaces the fence renderer, which is
      where a highlighter plugs in. Pass a stable object: a new one every
      render re-renders every block.
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-pre"><code>{`const components = {
  a: (props) => <a {...props} onClick={trackClick} />,
  codeBlock: ({ code, language, streaming }) => (
    <CodeBlock lines={highlight(code, language)} language={language} streaming={streaming} />
  ),
};

<StreamingMarkdown content={text} streaming={!done} components={components} />`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Accessibility</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It is deliberately not a live region. A container that announces every
        appended token reads a stream back word by word, or re-reads the whole
        answer. Announcing belongs to the transcript:
        <a href="/components/message-scroller/">Message Scroller</a> is
        <code>role="log"</code> with polite announcements, and this component
        sets <code>aria-busy</code> while <code>streaming</code> so assistive
        tech can hold its updates and read the finished text once.
      </li>
      <li>Headings, lists and tables are the real elements, so screen-reader rotors and table navigation work.</li>
      <li>A table wider than the message scrolls inside a focusable wrapper, so it is reachable by keyboard.</li>
      <li>Links that open a new tab say so, in a visually hidden suffix you can translate through <code>labels</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
